---
// /methodology/ (TECHNICAL-TASK §6.3). Parameters are read from today's forecast.json.
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro';
import { assetData } from '../lib/data';
import { date, number } from '../lib/format';
import { accuracyTable } from '../lib/tables';

const { forecast: fc, ta } = assetData('silver');
const p = (m: string) => fc.methods[m]?.params ?? {};
const arima = p('arima');
const rev = p('reversion');
const mc = p('montecarlo');
const accuracy = accuracyTable(fc);
const rules = [
  ['RSI (14)', '> 50', '< 50', 'overbought > 70, oversold < 30'],
  ['STOCH (9,6), slow %K', '> 50', '< 50', 'overbought > 80, oversold < 20'],
  ['StochRSI (14)', '> 50', '< 50', 'overbought > 80, oversold < 20'],
  ['MACD (12,26,9) line', '> 0', '< 0', ''],
  ['ADX (14)', 'ADX ≥ 25 and +DI > −DI', 'ADX ≥ 25 and −DI > +DI', 'ADX < 25: neutral'],
  ['Williams %R (14)', '> −50', '< −50', 'overbought > −20, oversold < −80'],
  ['CCI (14)', '> 0', '< 0', 'overbought > 200, oversold < −200'],
  ['ATR (14)', '—', '—', 'volatility only, not counted'],
  ['Highs/Lows (14)', '> 0', '< 0', ''],
  ['Ultimate Oscillator (7,14,28)', '> 50', '< 50', 'overbought > 70, oversold < 30'],
  ['ROC (12)', '> 0', '< 0', ''],
  ['Bull/Bear Power (13)', '> 0', '< 0', ''],
  ['SMA / EMA 5–200', 'price above MA', 'price below MA', ''],
];
---

<TextPage
  title="Methodology: How FinForecast Predicts Prices | FinForecast"
  heading="Methodology"
  description="How FinForecast calculates its price predictions: data sources, the three forecasting models, technical-analysis rules and measured accuracy."
  lead="How the forecasts on this site are calculated, where the data comes from, and how accurate the models have been."
  updated={`Model parameters below are from the ${date(fc.run_date)} run.`}
>
  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    Silver spot prices (XAG/USD) come from Alpha Vantage, one price per day, with history from June 2011. Alpha Vantage does not
    provide a daily high and low for spot silver, so we estimate them from the iShares Silver Trust (SLV) ETF on Twelve Data: each
    day, SLV's open, high and low are scaled by the ratio of the spot close to the SLV close. Indicators that use the daily range
    (STOCH, ADX, Williams %R, CCI, ATR, Highs/Lows, Ultimate Oscillator, Bull/Bear Power and pivot points) therefore use estimated
    highs and lows. Weekends are excluded.
  </p>
  <p>The data is updated once a day after the close. Each page shows the date of the last price used.</p>

  <h2>The three forecasting models</h2>
  <p>All models work on the logarithm of the price, so percentage moves are treated the same at any price level.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>ARIMA.</strong> A time-series model that captures short-term momentum and noise in the last five years of prices.
      The order and whether to include a drift are chosen automatically by the lowest AIC. Today's model is ARIMA({String((arima.order as number[] | undefined)?.join(',') ?? '')}){arima.drift ? ' with drift' : ' without drift'}{!arima.drift && ', so its forecast is flat: statistically, the recent data gives no reliable direction'}.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trend + mean reversion.</strong> We fit the long-run trend of the price over all available history and model how
      the price moves around it. When the price is far from the trend, the forecast gradually pulls it back. Today the trend is{' '}
      {number(Number(rev.trend_annual_pct))}% per year, the price is {number(Number(rev.deviation_from_trend_pct))}% above it, and half of
      that gap is expected to close in about {number(Number(rev.half_life_bars) / 261)} years.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Monte Carlo simulation.</strong> We simulate {Number(mc.paths).toLocaleString('en-US')} possible price paths using the
      average daily return of the last 10 years ({number(Number(mc.mu_daily) * 261 * 100)}% per year) and the volatility of the last
      3 years ({number(Number(mc.sigma_daily) * Math.sqrt(261) * 100)}% per year). The forecast is the median path; the range is the
      10th–90th percentile.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The <strong>consensus</strong> is the average of the three. Its range combines the widest band of the models (80% of outcomes).
    Monthly tables show the monthly average and the lowest P10 and highest P90 within the month. From 2031 only the two long-run
    models are used. Any model whose path becomes unrealistic (non-finite, or beyond 0.1× to 10× today's price within 5 years) is
    excluded automatically for that day.
  </p>
  <p>
    We tested Prophet, a popular forecasting library, and dropped it: on silver it projected the latest spike or crash forward
    for years, producing 2035 values anywhere between $0.30 and $221 depending on settings.
  </p>

  <h2>Accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    Accuracy is the mean absolute percentage error (MAPE): the average distance between forecast and actual price. Until we have
    30 days of published forecasts, it comes from a walk-forward test: the models are refit on past data only, at {fc.accuracy.consensus?.samples ?? 0} past dates,
    and compared with what happened next.
  </p>
  <DataTable caption="Average forecast error (MAPE), silver" columns={accuracy.columns} rows={accuracy.rows} />

  <h2>Technical analysis rules</h2>
  <p>
    Indicators are calculated on daily bars and on weekly and monthly bars built from them (the current week and month are
    included while in progress). Inside an overbought or oversold zone a signal is shown as such and counted as neutral.
  </p>
  <DataTable
    caption="Signal rules"
    columns={[{ key: 'n', label: 'Indicator', rowHeader: true }, { key: 'b', label: 'Buy', align: 'left' }, { key: 's', label: 'Sell', align: 'left' }, { key: 'z', label: 'Notes', align: 'left' }]}
    rows={rules.map(([n, b, s, z]) => ({ n, b, s, z }))}
  />
  <p>
    The summary score is (buy signals − sell signals) ÷ signals counted. A score of −0.5 or lower is Strong Sell, below −0.1 Sell,
    up to 0.1 Neutral, below 0.5 Buy, and 0.5 or higher Strong Buy. Pivot points (Classic, Fibonacci, Camarilla, Woodie and DeMark)
    use the previous completed day, week or month. Today's silver data: {ta.ohlc_basis === 'proxy' ? 'estimated highs and lows' : 'actual highs and lows'}.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Statistical models only know past prices. They cannot anticipate news, policy changes or shocks.</li>
    <li>Uncertainty grows quickly with the horizon; the ranges matter more than the point values.</li>
    <li>Nothing on this site is investment advice. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Methodology reviewed by <a href="/authors/margerie-holtz/">Margerie Holtz</a>.</p>
</TextPage>
